.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Xr openssl-ec 1
command processes EC keys.
They can be converted between various forms and their components printed
out.
.Sy Note
OpenSSL uses the private key format specified in 'SEC 1: Elliptic Curve
Cryptography' (http://www.secg.org/). To convert an OpenSSL EC private
key into the PKCS#8 private key format use the
.Xr openssl-pkcs8 1
command.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
Print out a usage message.
.It Sy -inform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM Ns | Ns Sy P12 Ns | Ns Sy ENGINE
The key input format; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -outform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM
The key output format; the default is
.Sy PEM .
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.Pp
Private keys are an SEC1 private key or PKCS#8 format.
Public keys are a
.Sy SubjectPublicKeyInfo
as specified in IETF RFC 3280.
.It Sy -in Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
This specifies the input to read a key from or standard input if this
option is not specified.
If the key is encrypted a pass phrase will be prompted for.
.It Sy -out Em filename
This specifies the output filename to write a key to or standard output
by is not specified.
If any encryption options are set then a pass phrase will be prompted
for.
The output filename should
.Sy not
be the same as the input filename.
.It Sy -passin Em arg Ns , Sy -passout Em arg
The password source for the input and output file.
For more information about the format of
.Sy arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -des Ns | Ns Sy -des3 Ns | Ns Sy -idea
These options encrypt the private key with the DES, triple DES, IDEA or
any other cipher supported by OpenSSL before outputting it.
A pass phrase is prompted for.
If none of these options is specified the key is written in plain text.
This means that using this command to read in an encrypted key with no
encryption option can be used to remove the pass phrase from a key, or
by setting the encryption options it can be use to add or change the
pass phrase.
These options can only be used with PEM format output files.
.It Sy -text
Prints out the public, private key components and parameters.
.It Sy -noout
This option prevents output of the encoded version of the key.
.It Sy -param_out
Print the elliptic curve parameters.
.It Sy -pubin
By default a private key is read from the input.
With this option a public key is read instead.
If the input contains no public key but a private key, its public part
is used.
.It Sy -pubout
By default a private key is output.
With this option a public key will be output instead.
This option is automatically set if the input is a public key.
.It Sy -conv_form Em arg
This specifies how the points on the elliptic curve are converted into
octet strings.
Possible values are:
.Sy compressed ,
.Sy uncompressed
(the default value) and
.Sy hybrid .
For more information regarding the point conversion forms please read
the X9.62 standard.
.Sy Note
Due to patent issues the
.Sy compressed
option is disabled by default for binary curves and can be enabled by
defining the preprocessor macro
.Sy OPENSSL_EC_BIN_PT_COMP
at compile time.
.It Sy -param_enc Em arg
This specifies how the elliptic curve parameters are encoded.
Possible value are:
.Sy named_curve ,
i.e.
the ec parameters are specified by an OID, or
.Sy explicit
where the ec parameters are explicitly given (see RFC 3279 for the
definition of the EC parameters structures).
The default value is
.Sy named_curve .
.Sy Note
the
.Sy implicitlyCA
alternative, as specified in RFC 3279, is currently not implemented in
OpenSSL.
.It Sy -no_public
This option omits the public key components from the private key output.
.It Sy -check
This option checks the consistency of an EC private or public key.
.It Sy -engine Em id
See
.Xr openssl 1 .
This option is deprecated.
.It Sy -provider Em name
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
See
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr provider 7 ,
and
.Xr property 7 .
.El
.Pp
The
.Xr openssl-pkey 1
command is capable of performing all the operations this command can, as
well as supporting other public key types.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The documentation for the
.Xr openssl-pkey 1
command contains examples equivalent to the ones listed here.
.Pp
To encrypt a private key using triple DES:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ec -in key.pem -des3 -out keyout.pem
.Ed
.Pp
To convert a private key from PEM to DER format:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ec -in key.pem -outform DER -out keyout.der
.Ed
.Pp
To print out the components of a private key to standard output:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ec -in key.pem -text -noout
.Ed
.Pp
To just output the public part of a private key:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ec -in key.pem -pubout -out pubkey.pem
.Ed
.Pp
To change the parameters encoding to
.Sy explicit :
.Bd -literal
 openssl ec -in key.pem -param_enc explicit -out keyout.pem
.Ed
.Pp
To change the point conversion form to
.Sy compressed :
.Bd -literal
 openssl ec -in key.pem -conv_form compressed -out keyout.pem
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr openssl-pkey 1 ,
.Xr openssl-ecparam 1 ,
.Xr openssl-dsa 1 ,
.Xr openssl-rsa 1
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Sy -engine
option was deprecated in OpenSSL 3.0.
.Pp
The
.Sy -conv_form
and
.Sy -no_public
options are no longer supported with keys loaded from an engine in
OpenSSL 3.0.
